%erg 0.1
Title: Harden the asset machinery against silently losing a tracked file
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-09-10T06:56Z HDMX-coding-agent created

2026-09-10T08:43Z HDMX-coding-agent note rewritten after measuring the sweep instead of inferring it. The original framing -- "five adopters are frozen" -- was wrong twice: four of the five upgrade cleanly via the stamp path and have now been refreshed, and the content they were said to be missing was never in the shipped asset. Scope corrected, and the ticket re-aimed at the defect class the author names: a tracked file must never vanish or revert without saying so.

--- body ---
## Context

An 11-repo sweep (2026-09-10) was read as evidence that five adopters were
frozen on old releases. Re-measured with `erg init --dry-run` rather than
inferred from hashes, that claim does not hold:

| verdict | repos |
|---------|-------|
| `unchanged` | archiveCIRED, zoteus-fts5, polycentric_activity, search-works-for-zotero, DOIfetch, git-erg |
| `would refresh` | padme, climate-finance-het, aedist, CNRS/html |
| `would preserve` | chemin-de-voix (no stamp) |
| `would preserve` | ImperialDragonHarness (genuine local edit, see 0277) |

The four `would refresh` repos were never frozen: `isCleanUpgrade` consults the
`.erg-assets` stamp *before* the historical-hash table, their stamp matched
their disk byte for byte, and `erg check` had been telling them to run
`erg init` since June. They have since been refreshed (aedist
MinhHaDuong/aedist-technical-report#1171, padme MinhHaDuong/padme#87, CNRS/html
on branch `chore/erg-refresh`). One repo, chemin-de-voix, has no stamp at all.

The other half of the original claim was also wrong: the 2026-06-18 revision
those repos were said to be missing does not contain the optimistic-ID
collision doctrine. Measured on the shipped asset — `gh pr view`: 0,
`optimistic`: 0, `clear of the frontier`: 0, `cross-pr`: 0. The whole delta is
one line, and it advises renumbering to the next free ID, which downstream
experience has since overturned. Staleness cost nothing here; the shipped
content is the real problem, and that is 0277's subject.

## What survives, and why it is the point

Strip the false urgency and a real defect class remains, which is what this
ticket is now for: **the asset machinery has several paths that can overwrite
or strand a tracked file, and not one of them is proved by a test.** A file
that evaporates quietly is the failure we cannot afford, whatever the release
history around it.

The known paths, with their evidence level stated honestly:

- **A. Stranded, and silent on every channel.** With no `.erg-assets` stamp,
  `isCleanUpgrade` falls back to `extraHistoricalHashes`, which is empty, so no
  prior release is recognized and the file can never be upgraded. The same
  absence silences the two channels that would report it: `assetDriftWarnings`
  returns nil early when the manifest is missing, and `erg update`'s post-swap
  hint is gated on `os.Stat(manifestName)`. Three silences, one cause.
  *Demonstrated* — chemin-de-voix, `would preserve` with no warning anywhere.
- **B. Silent revert.** `isCleanUpgrade` never checks which side is older, so a
  binary predating the stamp overwrites the newer asset and prints `refreshed`.
  *Demonstrated on a fixture.* Filed separately as 0279; listed here because it
  belongs to the same class.
- **C. The fix's own hazard.** A wrong entry in `extraHistoricalHashes` makes a
  genuine local edit look like a pristine old release, and it is destroyed with
  no warning. *Structural, not demonstrated* — and it is the reason this ticket
  must not be closed by hand-typing hashes.
- **D. `erg migrate` bypasses the 3-state check.** `migrate.go:261` calls
  `installAssets(root, migrateAssetPaths, false, false)` — `refuseDiverged` is
  false, so a divergent `AGENTS.md` is overwritten unconditionally.
  *Read in the code, NOT demonstrated*: a fixture with a local edit survived
  `erg migrate` because the run reported `already clean` and never reached the
  asset step. Whether the path fires on a store that genuinely needs migrating
  is open, and settling it is the first action below.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/manifest.go` — `extraHistoricalHashes`, `isCleanUpgrade`,
  `assetDriftWarnings`, `assetDriftSignal`
- `src/go/init.go` — `installAssets`, `initAssetPaths`, `migrateAssetPaths`
- `src/go/migrate.go` — line 261, the `refuseDiverged=false` call
- `src/go/update.go` — the manifest-gated post-swap hint
- `src/go/assets/AGENTS.md`, `src/go/bootstrap_assets.go`

## Actions

1. Settle path D first, because it is the only one whose behaviour is unknown:
   build a store that genuinely needs migrating, give it a locally-edited
   `AGENTS.md`, run `erg migrate`, and record what happens. If it overwrites,
   that is the most dangerous path in the set and it leads the fix.
2. Populate `extraHistoricalHashes` from git history **mechanically** — a
   generated list, or a test that regenerates it from `git log -- src/go/assets/*`
   and fails on divergence. Never hand-transcribed: path C is exactly what a
   typo produces, and a typo there is unrecoverable.
3. Apply it to every embedded asset (`.ergrc`, `spec-erg-v1.md`,
   `integration.md`), not just `AGENTS.md` — the machinery is shared.
4. Make the absence of a stamp visible instead of silent: `erg check` should
   say that a store has no provenance manifest and cannot be upgraded, rather
   than returning nil and passing.
5. Rewrite the dormancy note in `manifest.go` to say what the table is for once
   it is load-bearing, and what a wrong entry costs.

## Test

Red step, and it is the invariant that matters: a fixture carrying a genuine
local edit must be preserved by **every** path — `init`, `migrate`, and an
`init` run by a binary older than the stamp. One test per path, not one test
for the machinery. Today at least one of those three fails (B, demonstrated);
D is unknown, which is itself the finding.

Positive control before trusting any silence: a fixture at a known shipped hash
with no stamp must currently fail to upgrade. If it upgrades, the test is not
exercising the fallback and proves nothing about the table.

Third arm: a fixture with no stamp must produce a *visible* message, asserted
on content. Path A's defining property is silence, so an exit-code assertion
cannot see it.

## Verification

- [ ] path D settled and recorded, with the transcript in this ticket
- [ ] a local edit survives `init`, `migrate`, and an older-binary `init`
- [ ] a stampless store at a shipped hash upgrades, and says so
- [ ] a stampless store reports its condition instead of passing silently
- [ ] the table is generated from history, and a test fails when a shipped
      revision is missing from it
- [ ] chemin-de-voix upgrades cleanly once the table recognizes it

## Invariants

- **No tracked file is ever overwritten or reverted without saying so.** This
  is the whole ticket; every other line serves it.
- A genuine local edit is never clobbered by any path, including `--force`
  paths that must at least announce what they replaced.
- `erg init` on a pristine current asset stays a silent no-op.
- Stores written before the `date:` field keep working; absent provenance
  degrades to today's behaviour, never to a refusal.

## Exit criteria

- Every path that can overwrite an embedded asset is covered by a test that
  fails against today's code
- `extraHistoricalHashes` is generated from history, not maintained by hand
- A store with no manifest is told so, on a channel a human or agent will see
- The dormancy note reflects the table's real role

## Related

- 0277 — the shipped asset's *content*, which the sweep shows is the live
  problem where staleness was not. Its `Blocked-by: 0278` still holds: making
  an unrecognized hash a hard error before the table exists would fail
  chemin-de-voix.
- 0279 — path B, split out because it is self-contained and fixable now.
